1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,083
This video shows how to make a D F A as small as possible. The method is called D F A minimization.

2
00:00:07,533 --> 00:00:23,618
First we find out when two states are the same. Then we learn the algorithm: merge everything, then split when forced. We run it on a or a b star and on f optional g star. At the end we solve two past midterm questions.

3
00:00:24,068 --> 00:00:35,737
A lexer is compiled from regular expressions in three steps. Regex to N F A, with the templates. Then N F A to D F A, with the subset construction.

4
00:00:36,187 --> 00:00:42,182
The subset construction gives a correct D F A. But it often has more states than it needs.

5
00:00:42,632 --> 00:00:49,565
Step 3 is minimization. It gives a D F A for the same language, with the smallest number of states.

6
00:00:50,015 --> 00:01:00,170
Here is the D F A for a or a b star, from the last video. It has four states. B and C accept. D is the empty set, the error state.

7
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:05,761
Two of these four states could be one state. Which two? And how can you show it?

8
00:01:06,211 --> 00:01:19,971
The answer is B and C. Both accept. On a, both go to D. On b, both go to C. So nothing that happens after B or C can tell them apart. We now make this idea exact.

9
00:01:20,421 --> 00:01:39,088
When are two states the same? Not when they look alike. Two states p and q are equivalent if nothing that happens after them can tell them apart. Formally: for every string w, the run from p on w accepts exactly when the run from q on w accepts.

10
00:01:39,538 --> 00:01:46,727
So p and q accept the same set of suffixes. For every w, the two runs give the same answer.

11
00:01:47,177 --> 00:01:55,881
A string w that accepts from one state and not from the other is a witness. A witness shows that the two states must stay apart.

12
00:01:56,331 --> 00:02:13,249
Equivalence is an equivalence relation. It is also a congruence. If p is equivalent to q, then for every letter x, delta of p and x is equivalent to delta of q and x. This is why the merged machine is well defined.

13
00:02:13,699 --> 00:02:29,955
An exercise from the slides. Here is a D F A with four states, p, q, r and s. Only r accepts. Is p equivalent to q? Is p equivalent to s? For each pair, give a witness, or explain why there is none.

14
00:02:30,405 --> 00:02:32,047
Pause the video and try it.

15
00:02:32,497 --> 00:02:37,596
First, p and q. We look for a string that accepts from one and not from the other.

16
00:02:38,046 --> 00:02:47,689
Try the empty string first. With no input, p stays in p and q stays in q. Neither accepts. So epsilon is no witness.

17
00:02:48,139 --> 00:02:55,584
Now try b. From p, b loops back to p, which rejects. From q, b goes to r, which accepts.

18
00:02:56,034 --> 00:03:02,733
One run accepts and the other rejects. So b is a witness, and p is not equivalent to q.

19
00:03:03,183 --> 00:03:09,583
Now p and s. Here we cannot try every string. We must argue that no witness exists.

20
00:03:10,033 --> 00:03:18,758
On b, p stays in p, and s stays in s. Neither accepts. So any run of b's gives the same answer from both.

21
00:03:19,208 --> 00:03:29,000
On a, both p and s go to q. From that point, the two runs are in the same state. So they are the same run, and they give the same answer.

22
00:03:29,450 --> 00:03:37,045
So no witness exists, and p is equivalent to s. Both accept exactly the strings that end in a b.

23
00:03:37,495 --> 00:03:44,727
We cannot find equivalent states by trying all strings. There are too many. So we work the other way round.

24
00:03:45,177 --> 00:04:01,198
Step 1. Assume that all states are equivalent until they are proved different. Two states are already different if one accepts and the other does not. The empty string is the witness. So start with two blocks: r, and p, q, s.

25
00:04:01,648 --> 00:04:17,093
Step 2. Split when a letter forces it. On b, q goes to r, but p and s stay in the other block. If a letter takes two states of a block into different blocks, some suffix separates them. So the block must come apart.

26
00:04:17,543 --> 00:04:26,695
Step 3. Stop when no letter forces a split. What is left is equivalence. Here, p and s stay together, as we found before.

27
00:04:27,145 --> 00:04:43,316
Here is the same method in the form you run with a pen. Step 1. Draw one bubble for all accepting states, and one for all the rest. If there is an error state, it gets its own bubble. Here: the bubble r, and the bubble p q s.

28
00:04:43,766 --> 00:05:02,411
Step 2. Draw the transitions between bubbles, one per state and letter. The result is usually not a legal D F A. Here the bubble p q s has two arrows labelled b: one back to itself, and one to r. That is an ambiguous transition. It is orange.

29
00:05:02,861 --> 00:05:19,032
Step 3. Pick an ambiguous transition and fix it. Split its source bubble into the states that go one way and the states that go the other way. On b, q goes to r, but p and s stay. So the bubble splits into p s, and q.

30
00:05:19,482 --> 00:05:34,586
Step 4. Repeat until no ambiguity is left. Now every bubble has one arrow per letter. It is a D F A again, and it is the smallest one. When you do it by hand, you may choose which ambiguity to fix first.

31
00:05:35,036 --> 00:05:51,527
Here is the same algorithm as pseudo code. It is Moore's algorithm, from 1956. The machine M must be total and trim. Start with two blocks: the accepting states F, and the rest. Drop a block if it is empty.

32
00:05:51,977 --> 00:06:07,401
Then repeat. For each block, look at each state q. For each letter x, write down the block that delta of q and x is in. This list is the signature of q. Split the block into groups of states with the same signature.

33
00:06:07,851 --> 00:06:18,389
In the picture, q 1 and q 2 are in the same block. But x takes them into two different blocks. So they split. Stop when a round makes no split.

34
00:06:18,839 --> 00:06:35,309
Each block becomes one state. The start state is the block of q 0. The arrow from a block on x goes to the block of delta of q and x. The accepting states are the blocks inside F. There are at most as many rounds as states.

35
00:06:35,759 --> 00:06:39,876
Moore's algorithm needs two conditions on the input machine.

36
00:06:40,326 --> 00:06:59,931
First, the machine must be total. Every state has an arrow on every letter. The error state must be explicit: a rejecting sink, drawn like any other state. Without the sink, goes nowhere on f and loops on f look the same to the algorithm. We see this counterexample soon.

37
00:07:00,381 --> 00:07:12,413
Second, the machine must be trim. Every state is reachable from the start. No string can tell unreachable states apart, so they merge into any block, and they corrupt the answer.

38
00:07:12,863 --> 00:07:21,930
The subset construction gives you both conditions for free. The empty set is the sink. And every set it builds was reached from the start.

39
00:07:22,380 --> 00:07:30,636
Now minimize our D F A for a or a b star. It is total, because D is the explicit error state. And it is trim.

40
00:07:31,086 --> 00:07:40,537
Round 0. The accepting states are B and C. The non accepting states are A and D. So we have two bubbles, A D and B C.

41
00:07:40,987 --> 00:07:53,723
Draw the arrows. On a, A goes to B, in the bubble B C. But D goes to D, in the bubble A D. So the bubble A D has two arrows labelled a. That is an ambiguous transition.

42
00:07:54,173 --> 00:08:04,839
On b, A and D both go to D, so that arrow is fine. B C on a goes to A D, and on b it stays in B C. These are not ambiguous.

43
00:08:05,289 --> 00:08:12,265
Round 1 in table form. For each state, write the target of each letter, and the block of that target.

44
00:08:12,715 --> 00:08:23,041
A on a goes to B, in the accepting block B C. A on b goes to D, in A D. D on a goes to D, in A D. D on b also goes to D.

45
00:08:23,491 --> 00:08:36,120
A and D disagree on a. A goes to an accepting block, D goes to a non accepting block. The witness is the string a. So the split is forced: A D becomes A, and D.

46
00:08:36,570 --> 00:08:49,434
B on a goes to D, and C on a goes to D. B on b goes to C, and C on b goes to C. Both letters take B and C into the same blocks. So B and C stay together.

47
00:08:49,884 --> 00:08:55,260
Round 2. Compute the signatures again, with the new blocks A, D, and B C.

48
00:08:55,710 --> 00:09:06,377
B and C both go to D on a, and into B C on b. A and D are alone in their blocks. Nothing forces a split. So the algorithm stops.

49
00:09:06,827 --> 00:09:19,179
Each block becomes one state. The start state is A. A on a goes to B C, and A on b goes to D. B C loops on b, and goes to D on a. D loops on a and b.

50
00:09:19,629 --> 00:09:36,205
We have two states and a sink. The state B C accepts a b star. That is what a or a b star was all along. A python check ran the old D F A, the new one and the regex on every string up to length 8. They agree.

51
00:09:36,655 --> 00:09:53,849
Now the counterexample from the course notes. The regex is f optional, g star: zero or one f, then any number of g's. The subset construction gives this D F A, if we leave out the error state. A, B and C all accept.

52
00:09:54,299 --> 00:10:04,603
Merge the accepting states. All three states accept, so all three go in one block. The result is one state, with a loop on f and a loop on g.

53
00:10:05,053 --> 00:10:17,427
This machine accepts f f g f. But f optional g star does not match f f g f. The result is wrong. It accepts any number of f's and g's in any order.

54
00:10:17,877 --> 00:10:29,269
What went wrong? B and C have no arrow on f. Without the sink, goes nowhere on f and loops on f look the same to the algorithm. And they are not the same.

55
00:10:29,719 --> 00:10:38,167
Fix it: add the explicit error state X before you minimize. X is a rejecting state, and every letter keeps you in X.

56
00:10:38,617 --> 00:10:51,971
What happens to the D F A if it gets an f in state B? It rejects. We model this with an arrow from B to X on f. C on f also goes to X. Now the machine is total.

57
00:10:52,421 --> 00:11:01,467
This is the same rule as before. The subset construction gives the empty set as this sink. Keep it until minimization is done.

58
00:11:01,917 --> 00:11:07,890
Round 0: the accepting block A B C, and the block X. Now write the signatures.

59
00:11:08,340 --> 00:11:20,308
A goes to B on f, inside A B C. But B and C go to X on f. The block is forced apart into A, and B C. On g, all three stay in A B C.

60
00:11:20,758 --> 00:11:26,561
Round 1 makes no more splits. The result has three states: A, B C, and X.

61
00:11:27,011 --> 00:11:40,536
At the end, you can drop the explicit error state X again. The minimal machine is A and B C. A python check confirms that it matches f optional g star on every string up to length 8.

62
00:11:40,986 --> 00:11:57,178
Now the example from course notes section 3.6. The regex is d star, then dot d or d dot, then d star. Here d is a digit. This is the D F A that the subset construction gives. It has seven states.

63
00:11:57,628 --> 00:12:05,159
D, E, F and G accept, because they contain N F A state 14. A, B and C do not accept.

64
00:12:05,609 --> 00:12:18,857
Many arrows are missing. C, D, E, F and G have no arrow on the dot. Each missing arrow goes to the error state. In the by hand method, the error state gets its own bubble, X.

65
00:12:19,307 --> 00:12:29,675
Run the by hand method. Draw three bubbles: A B C for the non accepting states, D E F G for the accepting states, and X for the error state.

66
00:12:30,125 --> 00:12:46,210
The bubble A B C is ambiguous on d. A goes to B and B goes to B, inside A B C. But C goes to E, in D E F G. It is also ambiguous on the dot. We choose to fix d first, as the notes do.

67
00:12:46,660 --> 00:13:00,271
Split A B C into A B, and C. A and B can stay together on d, because both go to B. Now A B is ambiguous on the dot. A goes to C, but B goes to D, an accepting state.

68
00:13:00,721 --> 00:13:20,390
Split A B into A and B. No ambiguity is left. The minimal D F A has A, B, C, and D E F G, plus the error state X. Seven states became four, the same as notes figure 3.8 c. Moore's rounds give the same blocks. Python checked this.

69
00:13:20,840 --> 00:13:36,413
Now a past midterm question. Minimize the number of states in this D F A, and draw the result. All six states are final states. The question also mentions dotted epsilon edges, but this machine has none.

70
00:13:36,863 --> 00:13:38,506
Pause the video and try it.

71
00:13:38,956 --> 00:13:57,772
First check the input. All states are final. If we start Moore's algorithm now, there is only one block, and nothing can split it. The result would be one state with loops on a and b. That machine accepts b b. The given D F A rejects b b. So that answer is wrong.

72
00:13:58,222 --> 00:14:14,286
The machine is not total. State 2 has no arrow on b, and state 4 has no arrow on a. So add an explicit error state E. 2 on b goes to E, 4 on a goes to E, and E loops on a and b. E is not final.

73
00:14:14,736 --> 00:14:19,664
Now round 0 has two blocks: the final states 1 to 6, and E.

74
00:14:20,114 --> 00:14:29,245
Round 1. For each state, find the block of each target. States 1, 3, 5 and 6 go into the final block on a and on b.

75
00:14:29,695 --> 00:14:37,161
State 2 on a goes to 3, a final state. But 2 on b goes to E. So 2 has a different signature.

76
00:14:37,611 --> 00:14:43,009
State 4 on a goes to E, and on b it goes to 5. That is a third signature.

77
00:14:43,459 --> 00:14:51,651
E goes to E on both letters. So the new blocks are 1, 3, 5, 6, then 2 alone, then 4 alone, then E.

78
00:14:52,101 --> 00:15:02,447
Round 2, with the blocks from round 1. State 1 on a goes to 4, and on b goes to 2. State 3 does the same: a to 4, b to 2.

79
00:15:02,897 --> 00:15:14,247
States 5 and 6 go to 6 on both letters, which stays in the big block. So 1 and 3 differ from 5 and 6. The block splits into 1, 3 and 5, 6.

80
00:15:14,697 --> 00:15:18,473
The other blocks have one state each, so they cannot split.

81
00:15:18,923 --> 00:15:30,144
Round 3. 1 and 3 still agree: a to the block 4, b to the block 2. 5 and 6 still agree. Nothing splits. The algorithm stops with five blocks.

82
00:15:30,594 --> 00:15:49,175
Each block is one state. X is 1, 3, the start. Y is 4. Z is 2. W is 5, 6. And E is the error state. X on a goes to Y, and on b to Z. Z on a goes back to X. Y on b goes to W, and W loops on a and b.

83
00:15:49,625 --> 00:16:04,239
The posted solution draws the same four states X, Y, Z and W, without the error state. You can drop the explicit error state at the end. The slides keep it, so every state has one arrow per letter.

84
00:16:04,689 --> 00:16:16,913
Six states became four, plus the error state. A python check ran the given D F A, our result and the posted solution on every string up to length 10. They agree.

85
00:16:17,363 --> 00:16:36,179
One more past midterm question. Minimize the D F A for U or F or T, star. We built this D F A in the last video. It has four states. Every state accepts, and every state has an arrow on every letter. So it is total, and no error state is needed.

86
00:16:36,629 --> 00:16:38,271
Pause the video and try it.

87
00:16:38,721 --> 00:16:50,967
Round 0. All four states are accepting, so they form one block. The other block is empty, so we drop it. Every letter takes every state into this block. Nothing can split it.

88
00:16:51,417 --> 00:17:00,953
So the minimal D F A has one state, A B C D. It accepts, and it loops on U, F and T. This matches the posted solution.

89
00:17:01,403 --> 00:17:17,403
There are other minimization algorithms. Huffman in 1954 and Moore in 1956 used partition refinement. That is what we did. The naive way costs order n squared times the alphabet size.

90
00:17:17,853 --> 00:17:32,295
Hopcroft, in 1971, uses the same refinement. But he always splits with the smaller half of a block. That costs order n log n times the alphabet size. Production tools use it.

91
00:17:32,745 --> 00:17:52,927
Brzozowski, in 1963: reverse the machine, determinize it, reverse it again, and determinize it again. The result is minimal. It uses two subset constructions and no partition. In the worst case it is exponential. Janusz Brzozowski worked here at Waterloo.

92
00:17:53,377 --> 00:18:05,409
Table filling marks the pairs of states that a suffix separates, one length at a time. The pairs that stay unmarked are equivalent. It is the same algorithm, transposed.

93
00:18:05,859 --> 00:18:14,541
To summarize. Two states are equivalent if no string accepts from one and rejects from the other. A string that does is a witness.

94
00:18:14,991 --> 00:18:19,877
Moore's algorithm starts with two blocks: the accepting states and the rest.

95
00:18:20,327 --> 00:18:29,351
Split a block when a letter takes its states into different blocks. By hand, this is an ambiguous transition. Stop when nothing splits.

96
00:18:29,801 --> 00:18:40,147
The input must be total and trim. Keep the explicit error state until the end. Without it, the f optional g star example gives a wrong answer.

97
00:18:40,597 --> 00:18:54,144
Each block is one state of the minimal D F A. Check the result with test strings. Next video: why the minimal D F A is unique, and how to decide if two regexes are equivalent.
